---
title: Your Mortgage Renewal Is More Than a Rate — 6 Things to Decide Before You Sign
description: Renewal is the one time you can change almost everything about your mortgage without a penalty. Here are six things to decide before you sign your lender's offer.
publishDate: 2026-11-17
category: strategy
readTime: 6
---

Every week I talk to homeowners who are about to sign the renewal letter their bank mailed them. When I ask why, the answer is almost always the same: "The rate looked fine." Sometimes it is fine. But the rate is only one line in a contract you'll live with for the next three to five years, and it's rarely the line that ends up costing people the most.

A renewal is the one moment in your mortgage when you can change almost everything — the lender, the term, the amortization, the features — without paying a penalty. Treating it as a quick rate check wastes that moment. Here's what I look at with every client before they sign anything.

## 1. Start Four Months Out, Not Four Weeks

Your lender's renewal offer usually shows up a few weeks before maturity. That leaves you very little time to compare. Most lenders will let you secure a rate roughly 120 days before your maturity date, so that's when the real planning should start. If rates drop after you lock in, a good broker can often get you the lower rate. If they rise, you're protected. Starting early costs you nothing.

## 2. The Penalty Clause Matters More Than You Think

Most people don't plan to break their mortgage. Then life happens — a move, a separation, a job change, a better opportunity. Plenty of five-year terms never make it to the end.

If you're in a fixed rate and need to get out early, many lenders charge the greater of three months' interest or an interest rate differential (IRD). How the IRD is calculated varies widely between lenders, and on a big balance the gap between a "fair" calculation and a punishing one can be many thousands of dollars. A rate that's a few basis points lower is no bargain if the penalty clause behind it is brutal.

**Ask before you sign:** "If I had to break this mortgage in year three, how would my penalty be calculated?" If the answer is vague, that tells you something.

## 3. Prepayment and Portability: Your Flexibility Built In

Two features I always check:

* **Prepayment privileges.** How much can you pay down each year without penalty — as a lump sum and as an increase to your regular payment? If you expect bonuses, commission income or an inheritance, these limits matter.
* **Portability.** If you sell and buy during the term, can you take the mortgage (and its rate) with you, and how long do you have to do it? A portable mortgage can turn a big penalty into no penalty at all.

## 4. Pick a Term That Matches Your Next Few Years

The five-year fixed is Canada's default, but default isn't a strategy. If there's a reasonable chance you'll move, renovate, refinance or restructure in the next two or three years, a shorter term or a variable rate with a lower penalty may serve you better, even at a slightly higher rate. The right term is the one that fits your plans, not the one that's printed on the letter.

## 5. Amortization Is a Cash-Flow Lever — Use It Deliberately

Renewal is a natural time to revisit your amortization. Extending it lowers your monthly payment, which can be the right call if your budget is tight or if that freed-up cash goes somewhere working harder for you. Shortening it saves interest if you can comfortably carry the higher payment. What I don't want is for clients to drift into either choice by accident. Run the numbers both ways, then decide on purpose.

## 6. Look at Your Whole Balance Sheet, Not Just the Mortgage

This is the one most homeowners miss entirely. If you're carrying a car loan, credit card balances or a line of credit at much higher rates, your renewal is the cleanest opportunity to restructure. Depending on your equity and situation, that might mean:

* Consolidating high-interest debt into your mortgage at renewal (which becomes a refinance, so the numbers need to make sense).
* Moving to an all-in-one account like [Manulife One](../manulife-one.html), where your income sits against your balance every day and you can pay the debt down faster without a lower rate.
* Setting up a readvanceable structure if you're interested in the [Smith Manoeuvre](../manulife-one.html) to make your interest tax-deductible over time.

None of these are right for everyone. But you won't know whether they're right for you unless someone actually looks.

## How I Run a Renewal Review

When a client comes to me ahead of renewal, we go through it in about an hour:

1. Your maturity date, current balance, rate and the lender's renewal offer.
2. What's likely to change in your life over the next three to five years.
3. Your other debts, savings and goals, so the mortgage fits the whole picture.
4. A side-by-side of options from across my lender network: rate, penalty terms, prepayment, portability and total cost.

Then you decide. Sometimes the answer is "renew with your current lender, you've got a good deal." Often it isn't.

